<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Zanj state</h1><p class="st-meta">869 CE – 883 CE · capital al-Mukhtara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rebel state of enslaved East Africans in the marshes near Basra, which defied the Abbasid Caliphate for fourteen years and sacked Basra.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Thousands of enslaved East Africans, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a>, were forced to clear the salt-encrusted soil of the marshes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> for farming. In 869 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-muhammad-zanj-leader">Ali ibn Muhammad</a>, a man of disputed background who claimed descent from Ali, raised them in revolt, promising freedom and wealth. The rebels built a fortified capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtara">al-Mukhtara</a>, among the canals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a> region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rebellion became a state with its own leader, coinage and administration, exploiting the marshes to evade Abbasid armies. It drew on Kharijite slogans about equality, though former slaves acquired slaves of their own. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in 871 with great slaughter and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>, cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> off from the Gulf and its revenues.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Abbasid regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muwaffaq">al-Muwaffaq</a> and his son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a>, built a fleet and siege city opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtara">al-Mukhtara</a> and stormed it in 883; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-muhammad-zanj-leader">Ali ibn Muhammad</a> was killed. The revolt deeply weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate's</a> finances.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zanj-state.jpg" alt="Zanj state" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zanj_Rebellion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>869 CE – 883 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Mukhtara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zanj Rebellion, al-Mukhtara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=876&amp;focus=zanj-state" aria-label="Zanj state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5786 1000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zanj state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6106 1256h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6106 1256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=876&amp;focus=zanj-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

